                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">07/Jul/26</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-10000"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>Incident Report:(192.168.189.128) from 192.168.189.129 at <span class="error">&#91;Time&#93;</span>. Suspicious tool transfer detected via local staging server. Detected by Splunk (EventCode 11), Wazuh (Rule 60114), and host integrity monitoring. File landing identified on user desktop.<br/>
Active Response: Alert Generated. Severity: HIGH.</p>

<p>Time of Activity: <tt>2026-07-06</tt> at 11:20</p>

<p>Reason for classifying as true positive: Checked the telemetry logs in Splunk and confirmed that an outbound HTTP GET request was made to the internal staging asset 192.168.189.129 over port 80. Cross-referencing Sysmon EventCode 11 confirmed a file creation event writing 'soc_alert.bat' directly into the user directory. No legitimate administrative workflows account for this script execution pattern.</p>

<p>Reason for escalating the alert: Detected Ingress Tool Transfer behaviors bypassing static host prevention filters. The activity successfully maps to the MITRE ATT&amp;CK framework technique T1105 (Ingress Tool Transfer) with Sysmon Event Code 11 tracking file system anomalies.</p>

<p>Recommended Remediation Actions: Isolate the endpoint from the virtual switch, locate and permanently purge the 'soc_alert.bat' binary from the desktop, and run a full credential audit on the affected machine.</p>
        </td></tr>
            </table>
